{"name":"Carmel Woods","hex":"#8d6b3b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"carmel","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of caramel."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/carmel"},{"term":"woods","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of wood"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A dense collection of trees, usually one covering a relatively small area; usually smaller than a forest.","For chemical behavior purposes, trees in full leaf (coniferous or medium-dense deciduous forests)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/woods"}],"entries":[{"title":"Carmel Woods, California","blocks":[{"text":"Carmel Woods is an unincorporated community in Monterey County, California, United States. It is located adjoining the northern city limits of Carmel-by-the-Sea and adjacent to Pebble Beach. Carmel Woods was laid out in 1922 by developer Samuel F. B. Morse (1885–1969). It included a 25-acre (0.10 km2) subdivision with 119 building lots. Carmel Woods was one of three major land developments adjacent to the Carmel city limits between 1922 and 1925. The other two were the Hatton Fields, a 233 acres (94 ha) between the eastern town limit and Highway 1, and the Walker Tract to the south, which was 216 acres (87 ha) of the Martin Ranch called The Point."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Monterey_17Mile_Drive_3D_SL20_17R16L_small.jpg/330px-Monterey_17Mile_Drive_3D_SL20_17R16L_small.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carmel_Woods%2C_California","wikidata":"Q49328049"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Carmel_Woods_with_Jo_Mora_and_his_daughter_Patti.jpg/500px-Carmel_Woods_with_Jo_Mora_and_his_daughter_Patti.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Carme